<EXPERIMENT_PACKAGE><EXPERIMENT accession="SRX20570567" alias="GSM7439314_r1"><IDENTIFIERS><PRIMARY_ID>SRX20570567</PRIMARY_ID><EXTERNAL_ID namespace="GEO">GSM7439314_r1</EXTERNAL_ID></IDENTIFIERS><TITLE>GSM7439314: laneM141-Input_1_BKDL210007688-1a-AK2701-AK4510; Homo sapiens; OTHER</TITLE><STUDY_REF accession="SRP440726"><IDENTIFIERS><PRIMARY_ID>SRP440726</PRIMARY_ID><EXTERNAL_ID namespace="BioProject">PRJNA978600</EXTERNAL_ID></IDENTIFIERS></STUDY_REF><DESIGN><DESIGN_DESCRIPTION/><SAMPLE_DESCRIPTOR accession="SRS17874993"><IDENTIFIERS><PRIMARY_ID>SRS17874993</PRIMARY_ID><EXTERNAL_ID namespace="GEO">GSM7439314</EXTERNAL_ID></IDENTIFIERS></SAMPLE_DESCRIPTOR><LIBRARY_DESCRIPTOR><LIBRARY_NAME>GSM7439314</LIBRARY_NAME><LIBRARY_STRATEGY>OTHER</LIBRARY_STRATEGY><LIBRARY_SOURCE>OTHER</LIBRARY_SOURCE><LIBRARY_SELECTION>other</LIBRARY_SELECTION><LIBRARY_LAYOUT><PAIRED/></LIBRARY_LAYOUT><LIBRARY_CONSTRUCTION_PROTOCOL>SGC7901 cells were irradiated once for 400 mJ/cm2, and lysed in ice-cold wash buffer (1× PBS, 0.1% SDS, 0.5% NP-40 and 0.5% sodium deoxycholate) supplemented with a 200 U/mL RNase inhibitor (Takara) and protease inhibitor cocktail (Roche) and incubate on ice for 30 min. After centrifuge, RQ I(Promega) with a final concentration of 1 U/μL was added to the supernatant and incubate for 30 min at 37°C. Immediately afterward, a stop solution was added to the lysates to quench DNase. The mixture was then vibrated vigorously and centrifuged at 13,000 g for 20 min at 4℃ to remove cell debris. Then RNA digestion by MNase (Thermo Fisher Scientific) was performed. libraries were prepared with the KAPA RNA Hyper Prep Kit (#KK8541, KAPA) according the manufacturer's procedure. crosslinking-immunoprecipitation and high-throughput sequencing (CLIP seq)</LIBRARY_CONSTRUCTION_PROTOCOL></LIBRARY_DESCRIPTOR></DESIGN><PLATFORM><ILLUMINA><INSTRUMENT_MODEL>Illumina NovaSeq 6000</INSTRUMENT_MODEL></ILLUMINA></PLATFORM></EXPERIMENT><SUBMISSION lab_name="" center_name="Wuhan Union Hospital" accession="SRA1647957" alias="SUB13489837"><IDENTIFIERS><PRIMARY_ID>SRA1647957</PRIMARY_ID><SUBMITTER_ID namespace="Wuhan Union Hospital">SUB13489837</SUBMITTER_ID></IDENTIFIERS></SUBMISSION><Organization type="institute"><Name>Wuhan Union Hospital</Name><Contact email="kaixiongtao@hust.edu.cn"><Address><Street>Jiefang 1277</Street><City>Wuhan</City><Country>China</Country></Address><Name><First>Yao</First><Last>Lin</Last></Name></Contact></Organization><STUDY center_name="GEO" alias="GSE233931" accession="SRP440726"><IDENTIFIERS><PRIMARY_ID>SRP440726</PRIMARY_ID><EXTERNAL_ID namespace="BioProject" label="primary">PRJNA978600</EXTERNAL_ID><EXTERNAL_ID namespace="GEO">GSE233931</EXTERNAL_ID></IDENTIFIERS><DESCRIPTOR><STUDY_TITLE>Decoding target genes of ESRP1 by crosslinking-immunoprecipitation and high-throughput sequencing (CLIP seq)</STUDY_TITLE><STUDY_TYPE existing_study_type="Other"/><STUDY_ABSTRACT>RNA-binding proteins and their mediated alternative splicing play important roles in tumor cell invasion and migration. Here, we report that ESRP1 is a key regulator of gastric cancer cell metastasis. Overexpression of ESRP1 inhibits the invasion and migration of gastric cancer cells, in vivo and in vitro. Through crosslinking-immunoprecipitation and high-throughput sequencing (CLIP seq), we revealed that ESRP1 binding to the CLSTN1 mRNA and mediated its exon skipping, which may be a key mechanism for its inhibition of gastric cancer cell invasion and metastasis. Taken together, our data provide a molecular framework for the role of ESRP1 in gastric cancer development. Overall design: SGC7901 gastric cancer cell line with stable overexpression of ESRP1 was established, And then CLIP seq for ESRP1 was conducted.</STUDY_ABSTRACT><CENTER_PROJECT_NAME>GSE233931</CENTER_PROJECT_NAME></DESCRIPTOR><STUDY_LINKS><STUDY_LINK><XREF_LINK><DB>pubmed</DB><ID>38114495</ID></XREF_LINK></STUDY_LINK></STUDY_LINKS></STUDY><SAMPLE alias="GSM7439314" accession="SRS17874993"><IDENTI